INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti evidence batch: Rewards for Justice, DOJ, Rapid7, CIS", "text": "SRC-001 Rewards for Justice states Conti is also known as Wizard Spider and is assessed as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. It says online aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti; associated location Russia; associated applications Conti ransomware-as-a-service, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader. It reports first detected 2019, more than 1,000 operations, over 400 organizations worldwide victimized, over 290 in the U.S., and describes theft plus encryption and publication/sale of stolen data if ransom unpaid. SRC-004 DOJ page lists Conti ransomware cases: 3:23-CR-00092 defendants Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov; 3:23-CR-00088 defendant Oleksii Lytvynenko. DOJ says Conti affected more than 900 victims worldwide; September 2023 defendants charged with CFAA and wire-fraud conspiracies, and November 2025 Lytvynenko charged with conspiracy to deploy Conti; defendants presumed innocent. It says Lytvynenko pleaded guilty to Count 2 conspiracy to commit wire fraud on June 10, 2026 and sentencing scheduled September 10, 2026. SRC-002 Rapid7 reports Feb 27 2022 ContiLeaks released 14MB of chat logs in 393 JSON files, some encrypted and incomplete; logs included internal communications, tools, exploits, design processes, credentials, alleged infrastructure, and alleged source code. Rapid7 assessed leak scale suggested a senior member or coalition of disgruntled affiliates. Chats showed interviews, coding exercises, salary negotiation, products, a large Bitcoin balance, software development, testing against endpoint detection, phishing templates, control panel and infrastructure. Rapid7 assessed Conti remained active and significant after infrastructure takedown and observed resumption of normal operations March 9, 2022. SRC-003 CIS describes Conti as notorious RaaS using double extortion and apparently tied to TrickBot. It reports ContiLeaks chat archives from 2021 and additional 2020 chats; later reporting said platform shut down and rebranded into smaller units, and later its leak/negotiation infrastructure shut down. CIS reports leaked structure: Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, Penetration Testers/Hackers, external spamming; separate budgets; insider claimed over 60 people in July 2021. Product improvement included Cobalt Strike license, security/AV testing budgets, and Windows 11 focus. CIS/MS-ISAC says affiliates and initial access brokers let operators scale, offload intrusion risk, and focus on software development. CIS reports 22 Conti-related SLTT attacks from mid-2020 to Jan 2022, and open-source reporting tied Conti group to Ryuk, but these are reported assessments, not adjudicated facts."}
